{"name":"Wood Bark","hex":"#302621","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"},{"term":"bark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a short, loud, explosive noise with the vocal organs (said of animals, especially dogs).","To make a clamor; to make importunate outcries."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The short, loud, explosive sound uttered by a dog, a fox, and some other animals.","An abrupt loud vocal utterance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bark (botany)","blocks":[{"text":"Bark is the outermost layer of stems and roots of woody plants. Plants with bark include trees, woody vines, and shrubs. Bark refers to all the tissues outside the vascular cambium and is a nontechnical term. It overlays the wood and consists of the inner bark and the outer bark. The inner bark, which in older stems is living tissue, includes the innermost layer of the periderm. The outer bark on older stems includes the dead tissue on the surface of the stems, along with parts of the outermost periderm and all the tissues on the outer side of the periderm. The outer bark on trees which lies external to the living periderm is also called the rhytidome."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/%E9%BB%91%E6%9D%BE_Pinus_thunbergii_20211007185113_01.jpg/330px-%E9%BB%91%E6%9D%BE_Pinus_thunbergii_20211007185113_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bark_(botany)","wikidata":"Q184453"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg/500px-Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg/1280px-Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg/500px-Poskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Damaged bark of a cherry tree","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoskozena_borka_na_tresni_20210821_150436.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/BarkAndWire-0308.jpg/500px-BarkAndWire-0308.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/BarkAndWire-0308.jpg/1280px-BarkAndWire-0308.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/BarkAndWire-0308.jpg/500px-B